Exercise 6.25 asks that a projection pair \(g:\mathcal{D}\to\mathcal{E}\), \(h:\mathcal{E}\to\mathcal{D}\) satisfy \(g(x)\sqsubseteq y\) if and only if \(x\sqsubseteq h(y)\), and therefore \(h(y)=\bigsqcup\{x\in|\mathcal{D}|\mid g(x)\sqsubseteq y\}\) and \(g(x)=\bigsqcap\{y\in|\mathcal{E}|\mid x\sqsubseteq h(y)\}\), that the first of those sets be directed and the second nonempty, and that \(g\) send consistent sets of elements to consistent sets and preserve \(\bigsqcup\) of such sets, not only directed unions. Here \(g\) is `inj` and \(h\) is `proj`. `galois` is the biconditional: monotonicity of `proj` together with `proj_inj_apply` (\(h(g(x))=x\)) gives one direction, and monotonicity of `inj` together with `inj_proj_apply_le` (\(g(h(y))\sqsubseteq y\)) gives the other. `lowerSet` is \(\{x\mid g(x)\sqsubseteq y\}\). `proj_eq_sSup` shows \(h(y)\) is its least upper bound, because `inj_proj_apply_le` puts \(h(y)\) in the set and `galois` puts every member below \(h(y)\); `lowerSet_directed` uses that same element as a common upper bound still inside the set, so the set is directed, and `lowerSet_bounded` is the bound needed for the supremum to exist. `upperSet` is \(\{y\mid x\sqsubseteq h(y)\}\). `upperSet_nonempty` witnesses it by \(g(x)\), since `proj_inj_apply` gives \(x\sqsubseteq h(g(x))\), and `inj_eq_sInf` shows \(g(x)\) is the greatest lower bound, because `galois` places it below every member and membership of \(g(x)\) gives the opposite inequality. Those two formulas are the sense in which each map determines the other. By Exercise 1.27, consistency of a set of elements is boundedness, and `inj_bounded` sends a bound \(b\) to the bound \(g(b)\) by monotonicity. `inj_sSup` is \(g(\bigsqcup S)=\bigsqcup g(S)\) for bounded \(S\): the inequality \(g(\bigsqcup S)\sqsubseteq\bigsqcup g(S)\) is two applications of `galois`, reducing it to \(g(s)\sqsubseteq\bigsqcup g(S)\), and the other inequality is monotonicity of `inj` on \(s\sqsubseteq\bigsqcup S\). `example_625` is the conjunction of the Galois connection, the two extremal formulas, directedness, nonemptiness, consistency preservation, and bounded-sup preservation. **Theorem:** yes — each obligation of the exercise is one of those statements. **Proof:** yes — the arguments in `galois`, `proj_eq_sSup`, `lowerSet_directed`, `upperSet_nonempty`, `inj_eq_sInf`, `inj_bounded`, and `inj_sSup` are the comparisons just named.
